ha-agent (1.3.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A probe that sends a byte gets its reply. close() on a socket that still
    has unread data in its receive queue does not send FIN: the kernel sends
    RST, and a reset aimed at the peer discards the reply bytes already
    written. Every
    prober that introduced itself therefore got nothing back on a completely
    idle machine: echo | nc localhost 7777 measured 30 of 30 resets, with this
    host's OutRsts counter up 33 over exactly those probes, and a client that
    sends a byte and then half-closes (nc -N, and most monitors that say who
    they are) got 0 replies in 600. No load involved, so nothing about the
    accept queue explained it - and HAProxy's agent-check sends nothing, which
    is why not one release since 2020 noticed while every check by hand did.
    The agent now shuts its own half down after the reply, so a reader sees
    where it ends, and reads back whatever was sent before hanging up. Silent
    probes still cost one send and one read: 600 probes at 150 concurrent take
    the same 0.06s they took before, on all three shapes.

 -- Bruno Silva <avlis@onurb.com>  Wed, 11 Nov 2026 10:26:05 +0000

ha-agent (1.3.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A burst of probes is answered in full. Every accepted connection used to get
    a thread of its own, in front of a listen backlog of 5, so when arrivals
    outran thread creation the kernel dropped the connections: measured 465 of
    2000 probes lost at 300 concurrent, TcpExtListenOverflows up by as much.
    What the prober got was a connection that was never served, which is
    nothing back: nc prints an empty line and HAProxy logs an empty agent reply
    and treats the agent as failed. It depends on how loaded the host is, which
    is why it read as intermittent and why no test caught it in six years -
    HAProxy fires every server's agent-check at once on a start and on a reload,
    and a box doing inference drains the accept queue more slowly. The reply is
    now written from the accept loop itself, from bytes the sampler publishes
    once per REFRESH_INTERVAL, so a probe costs one send and nothing about the
    answer is worked out on a probe's account: same host, same minute, 2000 of
    2000 answered, at a tenth of the CPU per probe.
  * A probe is never refused. The cap added in 1.2.0 closed connections without
    an answer past 32 unanswered responders, which is the very failure this
    release is about: an empty reply costs whoever probes us more than waiting
    does, and there is nothing to shed. The listen backlog goes from 5 to 256
    too, since that queue is where a burst is meant to wait.
  * An unreadable /proc no longer burns a core. The failed-sample path retried
    immediately, because the sample sleeps inside itself and the first read is
    what fails when a prefix is unreadable: a measured 1000 ms of CPU per second
    for the whole outage. It now waits out the interval, and the lifecycle tests
    assert both that an outage is cheap and that the numbers come back on their
    own once the prefix is readable again.

 -- Bruno Silva <avlis@onurb.com>  Wed, 11 Nov 2026 08:48:23 +0000

ha-agent (1.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A fourth factor in the reported weight, for a host whose work is done by an
    accelerator rather than by the CPU: how much of the recent sampling the
    busiest device in the box was NOT busy. Off by default (GPU_WEIGHT=0): at 0
    no sysfs path is opened, so the answer is what CPU and memory say, exactly
    as before. The figure comes from
    /sys/class/drm/card*/device/gpu_busy_percent, the number that device's own
    firmware computes for its activity level, so nothing in the agent names a
    vendor, a card or a device node and no driver's tool has to be installed.
    A host serving work from an accelerator and polled on CPU alone looked idle
    next to a peer with room: with the factor raised, the box that is spent
    answers low and the new sessions go to the one that is not.
  * That factor is averaged over GPU_SAMPLES readings, default 3, before it is
    used. The device reports a snapshot of something that moves in seconds - it
    was measured going from 79% to 0% sixteen seconds apart while serving
    inference - and the reply is a weight HAProxy redistributes traffic on, so a
    single reading swings traffic on every poll. A sample that cannot read the
    device clears the window instead of smoothing across the gap, so the average
    is recent truth or nothing.
  * A host whose driver exposes no such attribute is scored on the factors that
    did answer: the missing one leaves the divisor, so the rest keep their
    proportions. It is not counted as idle, which would give a machine more
    traffic for a device it does not have, and the startup journal says what the
    agent could not see rather than leaving that to be guessed. A device that
    exposes no percentage anywhere - an NPU, on the hardware these boxes were
    tested on - is invisible to it and reads idle, so the manual page says
    plainly that the honest setting on such a host is 0.
  * GPU_WEIGHT, GPU_SAMPLES and SYS_PREFIX in the configuration file, the manual
    page and README, checked against the names in the program in both directions
    by the lifecycle tests, as the other variables are.

 -- Bruno Silva <avlis@onurb.com>  Tue, 10 Nov 2026 19:17:17 +0000

ha-agent (1.2.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The reply no longer loses its last character. The state file was read with
    its final byte chopped off on the assumption that it was a newline, so a
    file written by printf, by echo -n, or by an editor that adds no final
    newline sent "drain" as "drai" - and HAProxy matches that word exactly, so
    the drained backend went on taking traffic while the operator believed it
    was out of rotation. Line endings and surrounding blanks are stripped now.
  * A value that is set but empty means the default instead of a crash. systemd
    sets "PORT=" in the environment to the empty string and os.getenv hands that
    back rather than the default, so disabling a value in the configuration file
    stopped the agent with a ValueError; the fallback is logged. A value that is
    present but not a number, or outside its range, now stops the agent with one
    line naming it. The three weights are counts, so 0.5 is refused, and all
    three being 0 is refused rather than dividing by their sum later.
  * A sample that cannot be taken no longer takes the sampling thread down with
    it. It died quietly and the agent served, for the rest of its life, the last
    number it had ever computed - HAProxy balancing on a figure from some moment
    in the past and never told. The percentage is now simply left out of the
    reply, which HAProxy reads as having no opinion about weight and so keeps
    the one configured; the drain word is still honoured, because the state file
    is read outside the part that can fail; and the reason is logged once and
    not once per interval. Covered: unreadable or unexpected procfs, MemTotal 0,
    missing MemAvailable, two identical cpu samples.
  * The reply is never empty, including the first REFRESH_INTERVAL seconds after
    a start when no sample exists yet. An empty reply is an agent-check failure,
    and with the HAProxy defaults a restart of the agent could put the server
    out of rotation before the first sample landed.
  * Errors a listening socket raises because of other people's behaviour are
    retried: ECONNABORTED from a client that reset before accept, EMFILE/ENFILE,
    ENOBUFS, EINTR. They used to reach the same handler as a fatal error and end
    the process. A bind failure now names its cause - address in use, port below
    1024 while running as ha-agent, address this host does not have.
  * HOST accepts an IPv6 address and the socket is created for its family;
    passing one to an AF_INET socket failed at startup and took the service with
    it. Empty still means 0.0.0.0.
  * At most 32 probes are answered at the same time; past that a connection is
    closed without an answer, which is an honest agent failure, and writing the
    reply no longer holds the lock that the sampler needs.
  * The unit sandboxes the agent: NoNewPrivileges, PrivateTmp, ProtectSystem,
    read-only kernel views, restricted address families, namespaces, setuid and
    personality. It reads /proc, one file under /etc and a socket, and writes
    nothing, so this is the shape it already had, now written down.
  * ha-agent(1) and README say what the percentage is to HAProxy: a weight, as a
    share of the one configured for the server, where a larger number means more
    traffic and 0% acts as a drain. Both called it a load estimate, which reads
    as the reverse of what the number does.

 -- Bruno Silva <avlis@onurb.com>  Tue, 10 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000

ha-agent (1.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Move the program from /usr/local/bin/ha-agent.py to /usr/bin/ha-agent with
    a python3 shebang. A package installs under /usr; /usr/local is the
    hierarchy the system administrator owns and the Filesystem Hierarchy
    Standard reserves for exactly that. lintian treats a package writing there
    as an error. The previous package owned the old path, so the upgrade takes
    that file with it; dpkg may then note that /usr/local is not empty and leave
    the directory, which is correct - it is not the package's directory.
  * One unit file for both families: the agent's options are read from
    EnvironmentFile=-/etc/default/ha-agent and
    EnvironmentFile=-/etc/sysconfig/ha-agent. The 2020 .deb pointed at the
    first and the 2020 .rpm at the second, so no single unit worked on both,
    and a missing file aborted the start instead of being skipped.
  * Service account maintained with adduser(8)/usermod(8) rather than plain
    useradd: the uid comes from the system range instead of being whatever the
    default allocator handed out, and an account left by the 2020 package
    (login shell, home directory in the user range) is corrected in place. The
    uid and any files on disk are never rewritten.
  * Maintainer scripts drive systemd through deb-systemd-helper and
    deb-systemd-invoke. Installing into a chroot, an image build or a host
    without systemd no longer leaves the package half-configured, which is what
    plain "systemctl" calls in the 2020 postinst did (dpkg state "iF", and
    every later apt run failing with "No file name for ha-agent").
  * /etc/default/ha-agent and /etc/ha-agent.status are declared conffiles, so
    an upgrade keeps the local configuration and a drain state instead of
    silently resetting both.
  * StartLimitIntervalSec/StartLimitBurst moved to [Unit], where systemd reads
    them; added Group, SyslogIdentifier and dropped the redundant ExecStop.
  * The prebuilt .deb and .rpm are removed from the repository. They were built
    by hand, could not be reproduced, and a copy of the .deb that circulated was
    an HTML page saved from GitHub's file view, which apt rejects with "Invalid
    archive signature". Packages are now built from source by ./package.sh into
    dist/, which is gitignored.
  * A manual page, ha-agent(1), covering the environment variables and the
    drain file, replacing the drift-prone list in README.md.
  * Package metadata corrected: Section admin (was "custom"), real
    Installed-Size (was a hardcoded 1024), a DEP-5 copyright file and this
    changelog.

 -- Bruno Silva <avlis@onurb.com>  Tue, 10 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000

ha-agent (1.0.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * First release. Agent for HAProxy's agent check: answers each probe with a
    load figure weighted across CPU, i/o wait and available memory, plus the
    first line of /etc/ha-agent.status so a backend can be drained locally.

